{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's central formalization of 'safe' as requiring an 'empty set of state transitions' / 'null effect' misrepresents RFC 9110's actual definition. RFC 9110 §9.2.1 defines safe methods as methods that do not produce actions beyond retrieval for which the user is accountable — not methods with zero server-side state transitions. The standard explicitly notes that safe methods can still have side effects on the server (e.g., logging, caching). Because SRC_001's entire argument that 'safety forbids the very operation (state mutation) the log exists to perform' hinges on this null-transition mischaracterization, it fails to ground the distinction in the published definition the problem asks for. The target even self-flags this in a countercondition ('If RFC 9110's definitions are not set-theoretic in this way and the formalization is an imposed overlay, it may not match the standard's actual language'), conceding that its formalization may not correspond to the standard's real language — yet it builds its substantive conclusion on that unverified overlay.","counterexample":[{"source":"RFC 9110 §9.2.1","fact":"Safe methods are defined as methods that do not produce actions beyond retrieval for which the client is accountable; the standard notes safe methods can still cause server-side state changes such as logging or caching.","implication":"A safe method is not required to have an 'empty set of state transitions'; it is required to impose no user-accountable side effect. Therefore safety does not categorically 'forbid state mutation' — it forbids accountable mutation, which is a different and narrower constraint than SRC_001's null-effect formalization."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 fails to answer the problem's first core question: 'what precisely distinguishes an idempotent operation from a safe one.' Instead of drawing the precise distinction, it claims the two properties 'collapse into the same requirement' and are 'duals across abstraction levels,' which is a deliberate conflation rather than a distinction. RFC 9110 defines them as genuinely separate: safety (§9.2.1) constrains accountability for effects, idempotence (§9.2.2) constrains the effect of N identical requests to equal that of one. A PUT (idempotent, not safe) and a GET (safe, trivially idempotent) illustrate they are independent axes — they do not 'collapse.' By framing the answer as a duality/collapse, SRC_002 withholds the precise published distinction the problem explicitly demands.","counterexample":[{"method":"PUT","safe":false,"idempotent":true,"note":"PUT is idempotent but not safe — mutation that is repeatable, proving the properties are independent and do not collapse."},{"method":"POST","safe":false,"idempotent":false,"note":"POST is neither, confirming safety and idempotence are separate axes."},{"method":"GET","safe":true,"idempotent":true,"note":"GET is both, but for independent reasons — safety from no accountable effect, idempotence from identical-request equivalence."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}